🎯 Unix File Management Assignment
Scenario
You are setting up a workspace for a backend engineering project. Follow the tasks step by step in your terminal to complete the directory architecture, manage files, and clean up workspace artifacts.

Part 1: Navigation & Directory Creation
Locate Workspace: Display your current working directory path to confirm where you are starting.

Create Structure: Create a directory named project_workspace. Inside it, create three subdirectories at once:

src

docs

backup

Navigate: Change your directory into project_workspace.

Sub-directories: Navigate into src and create two additional folders: controllers and models.

Part 2: File Creation & Inspection
Create Files: Inside project_workspace/src, create two empty files: app.java and config.json.

Create Documentation: Inside project_workspace/docs, create a file named notes.txt.

Inspect File Metadata: Check the detailed file metadata (size, access times, inode, permissions) for app.java.

View Architecture: Run a single command from inside project_workspace to display the entire directory tree structure including subfolders.

Part 3: Copying, Moving & Renaming
Backup Source Files: Copy app.java from src into the backup directory.

Move Files: Move notes.txt from docs into project_workspace root directory.

Rename File: Rename notes.txt to README.md.

Part 4: Links (Hard & Soft)
Symbolic Link (Soft Link): Create a soft link named app_shortcut.java in project_workspace that points to src/app.java.

Hard Link: Create a hard link named config_hardlink.json in backup pointing to src/config.json.

Verify Links: Use the command that reveals file details to verify the link count and target paths for both created links.

Part 5: Cleanup & Removal
Delete File: Remove config.json inside src. Check what happens to config_hardlink.json in backup.

Remove Empty Directory: Remove the docs directory using the command specifically built for empty directories.

Recursive Removal: Force-remove the entire backup folder and all of its contents in one command.









Scenario
You are investigating a server log file (server.log) and modifying a environment configuration file (config.env) for an application deployment.

Part 1: Setup Workspace & Initial File Creation
Quick Creation: Use nano to create a file named config.env. Add the following three lines and save it:

Plaintext
PORT=8080
ENV=production
DEBUG=false
Generate Log File: Run this command in your terminal to quickly generate a 30-line dummy log file named server.log:

Bash
for i in {1..30}; do echo "2026-07-26 10:00:$i INFO User session $i created successfully" >> server.log; done
Part 2: File Viewing & Reading Operations
Dump Content: Display the entire contents of config.env directly to the terminal output.

Read Top Section: Display only the first 5 lines of server.log.

Read Bottom Section: Display only the last 5 lines of server.log.

Paginated Reading (Basic): Open server.log with a simple pager that advances page-by-page when hitting Spacebar.

Advanced Search & Scroll: Open server.log using the tool that allows backward scrolling and live searching.

Live Stream: Run a command that continuously watches server.log for new incoming log entries in real-time.

Part 3: Text Editing with Vim
Open Configuration: Open config.env using vim.

Edit Port: Switch to Insert Mode, change PORT=8080 to PORT=9090.

Add Parameter: Add a new line at the bottom: DATABASE_URL=jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/mydb.

Save & Exit: Return to Command Mode and save the changes while exiting vim.

Verify Modification: Concatenate config.env to confirm the updates were saved.

Part 4: Emergency Discarding in Vim
Accidental Edit: Open config.env again in vim. Delete a line or type random characters into the file.

Abort Changes: Exit vim without saving any of the broken changes.